1. Purpose of Agreement 

This agreement confirms the final state, condition, and handover arrangements of the capstone project artefact ReadAlert developed by the student group Penta Intelligence in collaboration with the industry partner Wayne Wood while completing IFN736 at QUT. 

2. Description of Artefact 

ReadAlert is a software module within the Huddle Up application, delivered as a functional prototype demonstrating five agreed components: reader profile management, author catalogue, book catalogue, reading records (user books), and book lookup with scan/log functionality. By Week 12/13, the prototype is expected to demonstrate the agreed functionality of these components. 

The proposed architecture comprises an HTML, CSS and JavaScript frontend, C# API endpoints, and SQL Server database operations using the supplied ReadAlert stored procedures. Data operations will be scoped by Tenant ID and Member ID and follow the supplied API contracts and development instructions. 

The project handover will include a testing and completion summary documenting verified functionality, incomplete features, known defects, configuration dependencies, and integration limitations. The artefact is intended as a functional prototype; full production readiness and complete integration with Huddle Up are not confirmed under this agreement. 

 

 

3. List of Components and Deliverables 

Software Components and Shared Resources 

ReadAlert Software Components: Source code and supporting files for all five components: Reader Profile Management, Author Catalogue, Book Catalogue, Reading Records (User Books), and Book Lookup with Scan/Log Functionality. Together, these components demonstrate the agreed functionality of the ReadAlert module within the  

Huddle Up application. 

Shared Frontend Design: Shared frontend files, including Index.html and readalert-theme.css, to provide a consistent interface and visual styling across the five components. 

Supporting Handover Documentation 

README / Setup and Running Guide: Provides an overview of ReadAlert, its purpose, prerequisites, database setup requirements, and configuration instructions. It explains how to start the API and frontend, identifies configuration requirements without exposing passwords or secrets, and outlines how to demonstrate the five components. It will also clarify whether the instructions support standalone execution or require installation within Huddle Up. 

Technical Handover Guide: Describes the project folder structure, the responsibilities of each component, the API routes and their associated stored procedures, and the handling of Tenant ID and Member ID for data operations. It also documents required dependencies and any modifications made to files supplied by Wayne to support maintenance and further development. 

Testing and Completion Summary: Records the testing activities performed, the actual results obtained, and the functionality verified. It identifies known defects, limitations, incomplete features, configuration dependencies, and outstanding integration work or decisions, providing a clear account of the artefact's condition at handover. 

The software components and supporting files will be organised within the designated GitHub repository and final submission package. The accompanying documentation will support installation or execution, demonstration, technical understanding, and future maintenance. The documentation scope and format will be agreed with the Industry Partner, and the final materials will accurately reflect the implementation status and known limitations of the artefact at handover. 

Components by Module 

Shared frontend design (Ghazal Abdolah Zadeh Javazm) 

index.html and readalert-theme.css, an HTML and CSS design used across all five modules. 

Reading Record (Ghazal Abdolah Zadeh Javazm) 

Frontend: a simple HTML and JavaScript page to list, add and remove a member’s reading records. 

API (C#): ASP.NET Core API with GET, POST, PUT and DELETE endpoints at /api/ral/v01/user-books, plus health and readiness checks. 

Database: the SQL Server build script (ral schema, tables) and the test data used by the module. 

Stored Procedures: Wayne’s ReadAlert stored procedures used by the API. All data access goes through them. 

README.md for the module, and a User Stories document. 

 

 

Backend Integration (Sude Karadeniz) 

Merged C# API endpoints across the ReadAlert Modules. 

Integrated backend functionality with SQL Server stored procedures. 

Integrated JWT validation and tenant/member context handling. 

Author Catalogue (Sude Karadeniz) 

Frontend: HTML and JavaScript files which provide an interface for the Author catalogue, including searching, displaying and editing author details in a table. 

API (C#): an ASP.NET Core API with GET, POST, PUT, DELETE endpoints, plus and endpoint which retrieves Authors from Open Library API. 

Database: SQL Server table ‘ral.ral_authors’, storing author information. 

Stored Procedures: ‘ral.ral_authors_CRUD_JSON’, supporting author data retrieval and CRUD operations through JSON. 

README.md and User Stories for the Author catalogue. 

 

Reader Profile (Amisha Patel) 

Frontend: HTML interface for viewing and managing reader profile information, including display name, preferred genres, preferred authors, alert preferences, bookseller membership preferences, and reader status. 

API (C#): An ASP.NET Core API with GET, POST, and PUT endpoints at /api/ral/v01/reader-profile for retrieving, creating, and updating reader profile information. 

Database: SQL Server table ral.ral_ReaderProfile, storing reader profile information. 

Stored Procedures: ral.ral_ReaderProfile_CRUD_JSON, supporting reader profile data operations through JSON. 

README.md and User Stories: Documentation for the Reader Profile module, covering its requirements and relevant implementation and setup information. 

 

Frontend Integration (Julia Iezzi) 

